# Patch series, applied in this order by core/build/apply.sh.
#
# One patch = one fingerprint vector. Never mix vectors in a single patch:
# at rebase time a conflict must be localisable to a named concern.
#
# Numbering:
#   0001-0009  infrastructure (config plumbing)
#   0010-0199  JS / DOM vectors
#   0200-0299  network layer
#   0300-0399  automation traces
#   0900-0999  branding, de-Googling
#
# Lines starting with '#' are comments; blank lines are ignored.
# A '!' suffix marks a patch as expected-to-conflict on rebase (touches a hot
# upstream file) so rebase.sh can warn early.

# --- infrastructure ---------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below depends on this. It carries the fingerprint config from the
# agent into the browser process and out to EVERY renderer, worker and worklet.
#
# Config travels in read-only shared memory; argv carries only an opaque handle,
# via base::shared_memory::SharedMemorySwitch — the mechanism Chromium already
# uses for field trials. Verified on M150: one value set in the JSON appears
# identically in the main frame, a Worker and three kinds of iframe, with zero
# cross-context disagreements.
0001-fp-config-plumbing.patch!          # content_main_runner_impl.cc moves often

# --- navigator & friends ----------------------------------------------------
# 0010 covers the Blink side plus the Accept-Language header, which is built in
# //net from a profile preference rather than from navigator.languages —
# measured, not assumed: patching only Blink left a profile set to en-US,en
# still sending "Accept-Language: ru-RU,ru;q=0.9".
0010-navigator-basic.patch
# 0011 owns user_agent_utils.cc entirely: the UA string and the Client Hints
# must never be applied separately, or the header claims one version while JS
# claims another. GetUserAgentMetadata() is the single source for the
# Sec-CH-UA-* headers AND navigator.userAgentData, so the override lives there
# and nowhere else.
0011-navigator-ua-data.patch!          # user_agent_utils.cc changes most releases
0012-navigator-hardware.patch          # first real consumer of 0001
# NOT NEEDED. Measured 31.07.2026: our build's navigator.plugins and
# navigator.mimeTypes are byte-identical to real Chrome 150's — 5 entries, same
# strings. Since Chrome 94 the list is hardcoded and the same on every install,
# so it carries no per-machine entropy at all. Patching it would make us DIFFER
# from Chrome, which is the opposite of the goal.
# 0013-navigator-plugins.patch

# --- layout & display -------------------------------------------------------
# Screen::GetRect() is the single choke point for width, height, availWidth,
# availHeight, availLeft and availTop — overriding them one getter at a time
# would allow an availWidth larger than width.
#
# outerHeight is DERIVED from the real innerHeight plus a configured chrome
# height, never set outright: innerHeight is the actual viewport and page layout
# depends on it, while outerHeight - innerHeight is the OS tell. Phase 0
# measured 143 px on a clean build against 87 px on real Chrome.
0020-screen-window.patch
# Width and overlay mode must move together: an overlay scrollbar reserves no
# layout space, so a configured width that layout never reserves is not a width
# at all and the page still measures 0.
#
# THREE themes and the choice between them, not one file. The note here used to
# say "macOS only for now, the Windows build needs the same two overrides in
# scrollbar_theme_aura.cc / scrollbar_theme_fluent.cc", and that was two thirds
# of the truth. ScrollbarTheme::NativeTheme() in scrollbar_theme_aura.cc picks
# between Fluent, Overlay and Aura, and ScrollbarThemeOverlay::
# UsesOverlayScrollbars() returns true unconditionally — so patching the two
# themes and leaving the chooser alone would have measured 0 on any Windows or
# Linux build with overlay scrollbars on, with both patched themes correct and
# unreached. The override sits at the selection point, because the guarantee is
# about which theme runs; spread across themes it is one statement per theme and
# the next theme Chromium adds carries none of them.
#
# `scrollbar-width: thin` reports TWO THIRDS, and the first version of this
# patch returned the configured number for both values. Every real browser makes
# the distinction — macOS keeps 15 for auto and 11 for thin in its own table,
# Aura multiplies by Proportion() — so a page that set one CSS property got the
# same answer from us twice and had us. Fixed in all three.
#
# STATE, said exactly. The macOS side is written, compiled and measured:
# core/verify/verify-0021.py, seven checks, auto 17 / thin 11 / none 0, an
# iframe agreeing, a width of 0 restoring overlay, and an unconfigured build
# reporting the host's own 0 so the others mean something.
#
# The Windows side is WRITTEN AND NOT COMPILED. scrollbar_theme_aura.cc and
# scrollbar_theme_fluent.cc are not in the macOS build at all — the output
# directory holds scrollbar_theme_mac.o and no sibling — so this code has not
# been through a compiler, let alone a browser. It is the first thing to run
# verify-0021.py against on a Windows machine, and it is not "done" until that
# has happened.
0021-scrollbar-width.patch

# --- graphics ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Noise is applied at READBACK, never at drawing — the canvas keeps rendering
# what the page asked for. getImageData() and toDataURL()/toBlob() go through
# different code paths but call the SAME function on the same absolute
# coordinates, so a pixel read one way matches the same pixel read the other.
#
# The perturbation is a pure function of (seed, absolute x, absolute y): no
# counter, no randomness. A detector reads the canvas twice and compares, and
# unstable noise is a stronger signal than no noise at all.
0030-canvas-noise.patch
# Intercepts once at the top of getParameter() rather than in each of its ~80
# switch arms: patching them individually guarantees some are missed at the next
# rebase, and one un-spoofed limit contradicts every spoofed one around it.
# readPixels noise lives here too — spoofing the renderer string while leaving
# the rendered pixels real would be pointless, the pixels are the harder
# fingerprint.
0031-webgl-params.patch!         # webgl_rendering_context_base.cc is hot!
# Limits are driven by the same SUPPORTED_LIMITS X-macro the getters use, so a
# limit Chromium adds upstream is covered the moment it appears in that list.
#
# Features may only be NARROWED, never widened: removing one is honest, because
# requestDevice() then fails exactly as it would on hardware that lacks it.
# Adding one would advertise a capability the driver cannot provide and fail in
# a way no real machine fails — worse than the leak it was meant to close.
0032-webgpu-adapter.patch!             # WebGPU is still moving fast upstream
0033-client-rects.patch

# --- audio ------------------------------------------------------------------
0040-audio-noise.patch
0041-speech-voices.patch

# --- fonts ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Filters at the fallback layer, not just the enumeration API — measurement
# through canvas bypasses any list-only filter.
0050-font-fallback-filter.patch

# --- devices ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Narrows the device list to the persona's counts. Narrowing only, like WebGPU
# features (0032) and fonts (0050): dropping a device is honest because
# getUserMedia then fails as it would on a machine without one, while
# advertising a camera that is not there fails in a way no real machine fails.
0060-media-devices.patch
#
# NOT NEEDED. Measured 02.08.2026 against real Chrome 150.0.7871.187 — the same
# version this tree forks — with a fresh capture of both browsers:
# baselines/chrome150-fresh.json and fury150-fresh.json. The probe covers 14
# mime/codec pairs through BOTH canPlayType and MediaSource.isTypeSupported,
# plus MediaRecorder's type list and a proprietary-codec flag. 28 fields. Zero
# differences. H.264, AAC, HEVC, AV1, VP8, VP9, FLAC, Opus, Vorbis, Theora — all
# identical, including the ones a Chromium build normally lacks.
#
# proprietary_codecs=true and ffmpeg_branding="Chrome" in core/args did the
# entire job, and enable_widevine closed com.widevine.alpha. There is nothing
# left to patch, and patching would make us DIFFER from Chrome — which is how
# 0013 plugins and 0100 CSS media features were retired.
#
# One caution recorded for anyone who revisits: a codec list on a given
# OS + Chrome version is the same for every user, so it carries no per-machine
# entropy to spoof in the first place. Narrowing it per persona would not hide a
# profile in a crowd, it would remove it from one.
# 0061-media-codecs.patch

# --- webrtc -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The most serious hole the product had, and it was hiding behind a switch that
# looked like the fix.
#
# launcher.rs passed --force-webrtc-ip-handling-policy=disable_non_proxied_udp on
# every launch, with a comment explaining exactly what it prevented. In M150 that
# switch is read by content/shell/browser/shell.cc:127 and by browser tests, and
# by nothing in //chrome. Chrome takes the value from a profile preference,
# renderer_preferences_util.cc:157, and only from there.
#
# Measured 02.08.2026, which is how it was found. Our build with a proxy set and
# that switch present, a page opening an RTCPeerConnection: types
# {host: 1, srflx: 1}, the srflx candidate carrying this machine's real public
# address. Real Chrome 150 does the same, and parity is no defence here — an
# anti-detect browser whose premise is that the site sees the proxy, handing the
# operator's real IP to any page with four lines of JS, is failing at the one
# thing it exists for. Every profile on the machine handed over the same address,
# so it also linked them all to each other.
#
# So: override the preference. THREE fields, because the policy alone is not
# enough — webrtc_ip_handling_urls overrides it per-URL (renderer_blink_platform_
# impl.cc:670-675 breaks out of its loop on the first match and discards the
# policy) and webrtc_local_ips_allowed_urls turns off mDNS obfuscation for
# matching pages, replacing the .local name with the real private address. Both
# are empty on our profiles; both are cleared anyway, because a patch whose
# correctness depends on a preference nobody has set yet is not correct.
#
# And a guard, because ToWebRTCIPHandlingPolicy returns kDefault for any string
# it does not recognise (webrtc_ip_handling_policy.cc:33) with no error. A typo
# would not fail, it would leak — kDefault is the setting that gathers
# everything. A value that asked for something and got kDefault is refused loudly
# instead.
#
# The resulting browser gathers NOTHING — no host candidate, no srflx,
# iceGatheringState "complete". That looked wrong, and it is not: real Chrome
# 150, unpatched, given only {"webrtc": {"ip_handling_policy":
# "disable_non_proxied_udp"}} in its Preferences, reaches exactly the same state.
# It is what the enterprise policy that exists to stop WebRTC IP leaks does on
# real corporate machines. Verified in core/verify/verify-0070.py, 5 claims,
# including that an unconfigured build still leaks — a control that passes when
# broken is not a control.
0070-webrtc-policy.patch!

# --- time & locale ----------------------------------------------------------
0080-timezone-icu.patch
# WRITTEN, after being wrongly retired. The reasoning that retired it was that
# --lang already sets ICU's default locale in every process, traced through
# chrome_command_line_pref_store.cc:50, render_process_host_impl.cc:3734 and
# chrome_main_delegate.cc:1449. The trace was right about Windows and wrong
# about macOS, and reading it was not the same as running it.
#
# Measured on a build of this tree: with --lang=de and the German resources
# present and loaded, Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().locale reported
# "ru" — the host's own language — and (123456.789).toLocaleString() came out
# "123 456,789". With this patch the same build reports "de" and "123.456,789".
#
# Shaped exactly like 0080, and called next to it: LocaleController is
# Chromium's own answer to "make every context agree on a locale", and it
# notifies each isolate and every worker thread. A locale set on the main
# thread alone is a contradiction a page finds inside a Worker in one line.
#
# The launcher still passes --lang. That is not redundancy: the browser's own
# UI language should match what the profile claims, and a German exit whose
# menus are in Russian is a seam of its own. --lang decides the chrome, this
# decides the fact a page can read.
0081-intl-locale.patch
#
# Where the profile says it is, taken from where its proxy comes out.
#
# NOT written against Blink, and NOT device.mojom.GeolocationContext::SetOverride
# (what CDP Emulation.setGeolocationOverride and WebDriver BiDi drive). That
# override freezes position.timestamp at the instant it was set and delivers one
# position per binding before going silent, which starves watchPosition() in a
# way no real device does.
#
# The seam is one layer lower: ContentBrowserClient::OverrideSystemLocation
# Provider(), wired into the in-process device service at
# content/browser/device/device_service.cc:109. A provider there sits where
# CoreLocation and the network provider sit, so the caching, the QueryNextPosition
# handshake and every failure path stay stock Chromium.
#
# THE PART THAT IS NOT OBVIOUS, and without which the patch applies, links and
# does nothing: on macOS and Windows the OS has its own location permission and
# Chromium asks it FIRST. GeolocationProviderImpl::OnClientsChanged returns
# before starting any provider while that status is not kAllowed
# (geolocation_provider_impl.cc:297-300), so on a Mac that has never granted
# this binary Location Services access, the custom provider is constructed,
# started, and never asked for anything — every call ends in "Timeout expired".
# So the patch also returns nullptr from GetGeolocationSystemPermissionManager()
# when a position is configured, which makes that constructor take its other
# branch and record kAllowed. Both decisions read the same predicate,
# FuryLocationProvider::ConfiguredFix: on Apple the platform provider CHECKs the
# manager is present, and it is only unreachable because a custom provider
# forces kCustomOnly. Suppress the manager without supplying a provider and the
# browser crashes the first time a page asks where it is.
#
# The provider does NOT gate delivery on OnPermissionGranted, though the header
# contract says to (location_provider.h:37-40). That callback traces back to
# UserDidOptIntoLocationServices, which only fires when a user clicks the
# permission bubble — a page granted geolocation over CDP never walks that path,
# so the latch stays false and nothing is delivered, forever. Chromium's own
# macOS provider makes the same call: CoreLocationProvider::OnPermissionGranted
# is "// Nothing to do here." The permission that protects the user is a layer
# above and untouched; the denied case is a check in verify-0082.py.
#
# It repeats every second, which is not a heartbeat for its own sake: Blink keeps
# one QueryNextPosition outstanding per frame, so a getCurrentPosition issued
# while a watchPosition is open waits for the NEXT update. Emitting once left it
# hanging — measured, then fixed, then measured again.
#
# On the jitter question the old note raised: no jitter, and that is what
# upstream does. NetworkLocationProvider caches by WiFi AP set and on a hit
# re-sends the identical coordinates with only the timestamp refreshed
# (network_location_provider.cc:283-289). A stationary desktop repeats itself.
0082-geolocation.patch

# --- permissions ------------------------------------------------------------
# Touches TWO files, and they must never be split. Overriding only the
# Permissions API produced query="denied" against Notification.permission=
# "default" — a genuine contradiction, and exactly the headless tell this check
# exists to catch. Found by measurement after the first version shipped.
#
# Substitutes only while the real permission is still undecided. Unconditional
# was the first version and 0082 is what made it a lie: with geolocation
# actually answering, a site could prompt, watch the user click Allow, receive a
# position, and read "prompt" back from permissions.query in the same page. A
# persona describes the profile nobody has answered a prompt in; it does not get
# to overrule an answer the user gave.
0090-permissions-consistency.patch

# --- css --------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT NEEDED FOR PARITY. Measured: zero differences across all 21 media
# features we probe. prefers-color-scheme is genuinely per-user and would let
# two profiles on one machine be correlated — but it carries about one bit, and
# spending a patch plus its rebase cost on one bit is the wrong trade while
# higher-entropy vectors remain. Revisit if a persona ever needs to claim a
# light-mode desktop from a dark-mode host.
# 0100-css-media-features.patch

# --- storage ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Derives the os_crypt key from the profile's own key instead of the machine's
# keychain, so the cookie ciphertext inside a bundle can be read wherever the
# bundle can be opened.
#
# WRITTEN. Three things it had to get right, each of which would otherwise have
# produced a patch that applies cleanly and does nothing:
#
#   IsCompatibleWithOsCryptSync() must be TRUE. The main cookie jar takes its
#   encryptor with Option::kNone and so follows precedence, but extension
#   cookies, the password reuse manager, the GCM driver and the device OAuth2
#   token store all ask with kEncryptSyncCompat — a slot only providers
#   answering true ever fill. False would leave all four on the machine key.
#
#   AES-256-GCM, not the AES-128-CBC the platform providers use. GCM is
#   authenticated, so a wrong key fails cleanly; CBC will unpad garbage and
#   leave the cookie store to notice via its SHA-256(domain) check — after
#   which it still deletes the whole eTLD+1 group.
#
#   The key travels on its own descriptor, never in the fingerprint config.
#   That config is republished into shared memory for EVERY child process
#   including renderers, and the renderer is the one process on the machine
#   that runs the visited site's code.
#
# What this patch does NOT fix: a key that is simply wrong still destroys the
# jar, because net::CookieCryptoDelegate::DecryptString takes no flags and has
# no way to say "unavailable" rather than "corrupt". That case is caught in the
# agent instead — it records the key's fingerprint beside the profile and
# refuses to launch on a mismatch, which is the only answer that leaves the
# data intact.
0110-os-crypt-key.patch

# --- misc js ----------------------------------------------------------------
0120-performance-memory.patch
# Was one entry for two APIs. They are two vectors and the house rule says one
# patch per vector — and the two verdicts came out opposite, which settles it.
#
# BATTERY: needed, and needed for a reason a parity check cannot see. Measured
# 02.08.2026 against real Chrome 150.0.7871.187: both browsers answered
# navigator.getBattery() with charging=false, level=0.60, chargingTime=Infinity,
# dischargingTime=34680 — identical to the second, because both were reading
# this MacBook. Parity is perfect. Parity is also worthless: every profile on one
# host reads the same numbers at the same instant, and dischargingTime is a raw
# seconds count that ticks down, so two profiles opened minutes apart report
# values differing by exactly the elapsed time. That links accounts that a
# separate proxy and a separate canvas seed were keeping apart.
#
# The tuple substituted is BatteryStatus's own default (battery_status.h:23-24)
# and what every desktop on mains reports — the largest population there is,
# carrying no entropy at all. A laptop left plugged in reports it too, so it
# contradicts no persona in the catalogue.
#
# The seam is BatteryDispatcher::OnDidChange, and the wrong seam is loud rather
# than quiet. Override the four getters in battery_manager.cc instead and the
# values sit still while battery_manager.cc:115-122 keeps comparing the REAL
# statuses and keeps firing levelchange and chargingchange off the host's cell —
# a page that listens and watches level never move has found something far
# louder than the leak. Substituting the status the dispatcher publishes leaves
# nothing to fire on, because there is genuinely no change. That is the last
# check in verify-0121.py and it is the one that distinguishes the two.
#
# Through the BatteryStatus constructor rather than into battery_status_, so the
# level goes through EnsureTwoSignificantDigits exactly as a real one does: a
# value that skipped it could report a level no Chrome can produce, which is
# evidence of tampering rather than a failed disguise.
0121-battery-status.patch
#
# NETWORK INFORMATION: NOT NEEDED, and this one is a finding rather than a
# shortcut. rtt and downlink are not leaking the host — they are Chrome's own
# designed noise. GetRandomMultiplier (network_state_notifier.cc:370-383) draws
# a per-renderer-process salt once and multiplies both by one of 21 values in
# [0.90, 1.10], then rounds to 50 ms and 50 kbps buckets. Across all 29 captures
# in tools/detect-suite/baselines, downlink takes exactly seven values (1.45
# through 1.75 in 0.05 steps) and rtt takes 100, 150 or 200; effectiveType is
# "4g" in every single one, ours, Chrome's and AdsPower's alike. Real Chrome's
# 1.5 and our 1.7 are two draws from one distribution.
#
# Pinning them per profile would REMOVE variance that real Chrome has: a profile
# whose navigator.connection.rtt is identical across reloads while Chrome's moves
# is a NEW tell, which is the argument verbatim that retired 0200/0210/0220 for
# JA3. Same reasoning disposes of --force-effective-connection-type.
#
# If it is ever revived: NetworkInformation is Exposed=(Window,Worker), so a
# patch would have to cover workers or produce exactly the cross-context
# disagreement probe.js exists to catch — a cost battery does not carry, since
# BatteryManager is Window-only.
# 0122-network-information.patch

# --- network ----------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT NEEDED FOR PARITY, and removing them from the plan is a finding rather
# than a shortcut. Verified against M150 source on 31.07.2026:
#
#   SSL_set_permute_extensions(ssl_, 1)   extension order is randomised per
#                                         connection, so real Chrome has no
#                                         stable JA3 to match
#   SSL_CTX_set_grease_enabled(ctx_, 1)   further per-connection variation
#   SSL_CTX_set_cipher_list                never called — BoringSSL defaults
#
# Our build is the same Chromium with the same BoringSSL on the same code path,
# so its ClientHello is already indistinguishable from Chrome 150's. Pinning a
# JA3 per profile would make us LESS Chrome-like, not more.
#
# These become necessary only to impersonate a different browser or major
# version, which is a separate goal from parity. The same argument applies
# verbatim to HTTP/2 SETTINGS and header order.
#
# 0200-tls-ja3.patch
# 0210-http2-settings.patch
# 0220-header-order.patch
#
# The real network work is NOT in the core: it is QUIC and WebRTC through the
# proxy relay (agent/src/relay.rs). Chrome uses QUIC; launcher.rs currently
# passes --disable-quic because UDP would bypass an HTTP relay, and that is our
# own deviation from Chrome. Closing it needs SOCKS5 UDP ASSOCIATE in the relay,
# not a Chromium patch. See docs/05.
# NOT A PATCH, and the reasoning matters more than the outcome.
#
# --disable-quic in launcher.rs already lands on the identical field a patch
# would set, so a patch here is redundant rather than wrong. What must NOT be
# recorded is "we are at parity". We are not: real Chrome 150 negotiates HTTP/3
# with any server that offers it, and Fury never does. That is a real, unfixed
# deviation, visible server-side to anyone advertising alt-svc and watching
# whether the client ever upgrades.
#
# It is owned by the proxy architecture, not by the core. relay.rs speaks HTTP
# CONNECT and SOCKS5 CONNECT, both TCP; QUIC is UDP and would go around it,
# which is the same hole 0070 just closed for WebRTC.
#
# And the exit is not blocked by design, which an earlier note claimed. QUIC
# proxying is gated on ONE GN default: net/features.gni:70 sets
# enable_quic_proxy_support = is_debug, and our args set is_debug = false. The
# MASQUE client (RFC 9298, UDP over HTTP/3) is compiled into the binary
# unconditionally — net/BUILD.gn lists quic_proxy_datagram_client_socket.{cc,h}
# with no buildflag guard — and pref_proxy_config_tracker_impl.cc already passes
# allow_quic_proxy_support through conditionally. So the road out is a GN arg
# plus a MASQUE-capable relay, not a Chromium patch and not SOCKS5 UDP ASSOCIATE.
# 0230-disable-quic.patch

# --- automation traces ------------------------------------------------------
0300-remove-cdc-vars.patch
# NOT WRITTEN, and blocked on a boundary rather than on effort.
#
# What was measured first, because three of the four candidate tells turned out
# not to exist. On M150, with and without Runtime.enable, in our build AND in
# real Chrome 150: a getter on a logged object never fires, a getter on
# Error.prototype.stack never fires, and console.log of a regexp calls toString
# in every configuration including no-CDP — so it is not a CDP tell at all. The
# classic "Runtime.enable detection" does not reproduce.
#
# What DOES reproduce is timing, and it was measured again on 04.08.2026 in more
# detail, because "about thirteen times" is a number you cannot act on. Fury 150,
# microseconds per console.debug, median of seven runs of four hundred
# (tools/detect-suite/cdp-timing.py):
#
#                                "hello"   50-key   800-key
#     no debugger                   3.0      2.8      2.8
#     CDP attached, Runtime off     2.8      2.8      2.8
#     CDP attached, Runtime on      8.0     14.0     25.5
#
# Three things that the single ratio hid.
#
# Attaching is free. The whole cost arrives with Runtime.enable, which every
# driver calls — so what leaks is "a driver is attached", not "a debugger is".
#
# The cost is a FIXED part plus a SIZE part. The size part is preview generation
# in wrapArguments(session, generatePreview) and a patch could remove it. The
# fixed part — 2.7x on a five-character string — is the message reaching the
# frontend at all, and removing that means not delivering
# Runtime.consoleAPICalled, which is the console.
#
# So a patch here shrinks the tell and cannot close it, and a patch that applies
# and still leaks is the exact failure mode this file exists to prevent. Not
# written. Identical in real Chrome, so it detects a driven browser rather than
# Fury — which is no comfort when the driven browser is the operator's.
#
# The one substantive leak found is narrower: console serialisation of an Error
# reads .stack, which runs the page's Error.prepareStackTrace. It cannot be
# closed from Blink. The preview path reads the property through
# value-mirror.cc's own object->Get, which no embedder hook intercepts — a
# Blink-side valueSubtype override covers descriptionForError and NOT the
# preview, so a patch there applies, compiles, links and still leaks. The only
# layer covering both readers is v8/src/inspector, and //v8 is a separate
# gclient project with no path to //components/fury: fury::FuryConfig::Get()
# would not link, and no patch in this series touches v8/ today.
#
# So it needs either a v8 patch with its own config channel, or a decision that
# an operator driving a profile over CDP accepts a timing tell. Written down
# rather than half-done: a patch that applies and leaks is the failure mode this
# file exists to prevent.
# 0301-runtime-enable-detect.patch
# Until this patch, LaunchRestrictions::lock_devtools was a hidden button.
# launcher.rs pushed --fury-lock-devtools on every restricted launch; grep over
# core/src returned zero occurrences of it. docs/04 insists the restriction must
# be technical rather than a UI choice, and it was a UI choice — an operator
# forbidden from taking a profile's data home could open DevTools and read the
# cookies out of it.
#
# FIVE guard points, and the first version had two. The two it had —
# GetEffectiveAvailability and GetDevToolsAvailabilityForUrl — passed a test
# that showed CDP being refused, and left the profile-file bypass wide open:
# IsInspectionAllowed(profile, extension=nullptr), on kDisallowed, reads
# prefs::kDeveloperToolsAvailabilityAllowlist STRAIGHT off the profile and
# returns true when it is non-empty, never touching the policy checker. That
# preference lives in the Preferences JSON inside the user-data-dir — the one
# file the restricted operator is certain to have. One line in it and DevTools
# came back. So the guard now sits on all five IsInspectionAllowed entry points,
# ahead of any preference, and the bypass is a check in verify-0302.py: launch
# locked, write {"devtools": {"availability_allowlist": ["*"]}} into the profile,
# and it must still refuse.
#
# Enforced at the READ, not by seeding prefs::kDevToolsAvailability: that
# preference lives in the Preferences JSON inside the user-data-dir, which is
# exactly what the restricted operator has a copy of. argv cannot be changed
# after the browser starts; a file in the profile can be edited between
# launches.
#
# It lands in DevToolsAgentHostImpl::AttachInternal, which every session goes
# through — so one guard covers --remote-debugging-port, --remote-debugging-pipe
# and chrome.debugger together, and the endpoint refusing per-attach rather than
# refusing to listen is the same shape Chrome's own policy produces.
#
# What it does NOT stop, said plainly because an undocumented limit gets trusted
# past: an operator who has the binary can relaunch it by hand without the
# switch. That is the threat model in docs/04 — a dishonest employee with a
# profile, not one with root on the machine.
#
# The switch string is written independently in Rust and in C++ and nothing
# checks the two against each other, so verify-0302.py spells it the Rust way on
# purpose. A drifted name fails open, silently, forever.
0302-devtools-lock.patch               # honours LaunchRestrictions.lock_devtools
# Split out of 0302 rather than riding inside it: different vector, disjoint
# files, and one patch per vector is what keeps a rebase conflict localisable.
# lock_data_export was the other switch launcher.rs pushed into the void.
#
# THREE refusals, and the third is what makes the first two worth having. The
# CSV export (PasswordManagerPorter::Export) and the bookmark HTML export
# (bookmark_html_writer::WriteBookmarks) are the obvious bulk paths, and each is
# refused at the function that does the work rather than at the button that
# starts it — the settings page reaches both over Mojo, and a replayed message
# or an extension holding the permission reaches them with no button at all.
#
# The third is PasswordsPrivateDelegateImpl::RequestPlaintextPassword. Reading
# credentials out one at a time is the same leak as exporting them in a batch,
# only slower, and it is reachable from chrome://settings whether or not
# chrome://password-manager is disabled — _api_features.json exposes
# passwordsPrivate to both hosts. Blocking the WebUI host alone would have
# demoed perfectly and protected nothing.
#
# NOT VERIFIED on a running browser, and that is a gap rather than an omission:
# all three paths are WebUI or extension surfaces a page cannot reach, so
# core/verify has no way to drive them over CDP today. The build proves the
# refusals compile into the binary; nothing yet proves they fire. Deliberately
# not claimed as done.
#
# The file-download whitelist clause in docs/04 is NOT covered here. It is a
# DownloadPrefs / download-target problem and belongs to its own number.
0303-data-export-lock.patch

# --- branding ---------------------------------------------------------------
# WRITTEN and measured — see the paragraph beginning "WRITTEN." below, which is
# the current state. This opening said "NOT WRITTEN, blocked on assets" for as
# long as it took the icons to arrive, and then stayed after they did: the first
# thing a reader saw contradicted the same block twelve lines down. Kept as
# background rather than deleted, because the reasoning still explains why the
# patch is ten lines of a text file.
#
# The change is data, not source: chrome/app/theme/chromium/BRANDING is read by
# build/util/branding.gni into chrome_product_full_name and the mac bundle id,
# and everything macOS-facing derives from those — the .app, the .framework, the
# helper bundles, CFBundleIdentifier, the runtime framework lookup, the crash
# annotation. No file in chrome/BUILD.gn, app-Info.plist or chrome_constants.cc
# needs touching. A second edit to chromium_strings.grd covers the About box and
# chrome://version.
#
# What blocks it: the icons. chrome/BUILD.gn bundles app.icns AND Assets.car AND
# Assets.xcassets/AppIcon.icon, and shipping a rename with Chromium's own icons
# is worse than not renaming. Those are a design deliverable.
#
# THE RULE THAT GOVERNS THIS PATCH, written here so it cannot be lost: nothing a
# PAGE can read may move. The user agent, navigator.appName, navigator.vendor
# and the Sec-CH-UA brand list belong to 0011 and must keep saying Chrome.
# Branding here means what the USER and the OPERATING SYSTEM see, and nothing
# else. A rename that reaches a page is a detection vector, not branding.
#
# And a trap worth knowing before the first attempt: ninja does not delete
# Chromium.app when the output name changes, so both bundles sit in the output
# directory afterwards and anyone testing the old path reports that the patch
# did nothing.
#
# WRITTEN. Ten lines of a text file, and everything macOS-facing follows:
# build/util/branding.gni reads chrome/app/theme/chromium/BRANDING into
# chrome_product_full_name, chrome_mac_bundle_id and chrome_mac_creator_code,
# and chrome/BUILD.gn derives the .app name, the framework name, the four helper
# bundles and CFBundleIdentifier from those. No source file needs touching.
# Measured after building: Fury.app, Fury Framework.framework, four Fury Helper
# bundles, CFBundleName "Fury", CFBundleIdentifier dev.fury.core.
#
# THE ICONS ARE NOT IN THIS PATCH. They are PNGs and an .icns, and a quilt
# series carrying binaries is one nobody can read or rebase — the same argument
# link-widevine.sh makes for the CDM. core/build/link-icons.sh copies them out
# of assets/ before a build, and skipping it produces a browser called Fury
# wearing Chromium's icons, which is worse than either.
#
# NOTHING A PAGE CAN READ MOVES, and that was checked rather than assumed. The
# User-Agent product string is a hardcoded literal — version_info_with_user_agent
# .h:21 returns "Chrome/" PRODUCT_VERSION — so the rename cannot reach it. The
# Client Hints brand at user_agent_utils.cc:169 DOES read
# version_info::GetProductName(), and it sits behind `#if !BUILDFLAG(
# CHROMIUM_BRANDING)`, which is off in our build; patch 0011 replaces the whole
# brand list from the config regardless.
#
# The trap this note existed to warn about is real: ninja does not delete
# Chromium.app when the output name changes, so both bundles sit in the output
# directory afterwards. Confirmed, and removed by hand.
#
# VERIFIED on the renamed build: eight contexts, zero disagreements, and the
# release gate passes 13/13. navigator.userAgent still says Chrome/150.0.0.0 and
# the brand list still says "Not;A=Brand/8,Chromium/150,Google Chrome/150" — the
# rename reaches the bundle and stops there, which is the whole requirement.
#
# The first attempt at this failed with origin "null" and isSecureContext false
# in every worker, which looked like the rename having broken something. It had
# not: the collector serving probe.html was not listening at that moment, so the
# browser was sitting on an error page, whose origin is null and which is not a
# secure context — hence no navigator.serviceWorker and SecurityError from every
# blob worker. Chasing it cost an hour, and the lesson is narrow and repeatable:
# an "origin: null" in a capture means the page did not load, and the first
# thing to check is the thing serving it.
0900-branding.patch
#
# ONE LINE, and it closes the last endpoint the browser contacts on its own.
#
# Measured against the launch flags we already pass: --disable-background-
# networking stops the Safe Browsing DATABASE downloading
# (v4_update_protocol_manager.cc:106-109), but the per-download client report
# still goes to a hardcoded sb-ssl.google.com URL that no API key gates. So the
# browser has been protecting nothing while chrome://settings reported Standard
# protection and while the pings kept leaving. Defaulting kSafeBrowsingEnabled
# to false removes the traffic and the contradiction together.
#
# The pref and NOT the URL constant: download_protection_delegate_desktop.cc
# CHECKs the constructed URL is valid, so blanking the constant crashes the
# browser at startup. The pref is also the one gate that client-side phishing,
# the ping manager, notification telemetry and password protection all read.
#
# It sets a DEFAULT. A profile whose Preferences already carry
# safebrowsing.enabled keeps its own value — Fury creates profiles fresh, so the
# default is what every new one gets.
0901-disable-google-services.patch
